<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tamale</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tamale is the capital of the Northern Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> and the largest city of northern Ghana. It became the administrative centre of the British Northern Territories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> in 1907, replacing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gambaga">Gambaga</a>, and grew as a market and route centre. Most of its people are Dagomba, and it is a centre of Islamic learning.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shitobu's son Naa Nyagsi (r. 1416–1432) waged a war of expansion, overthrew many of the Tindaamba, set his sons, brothers and uncles over the conquered Konkomba, Nafeba, Basare and Chamba, and made his capital at Yani Dabari near <b>Tamale</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-dagbon">Kingdom of Dagbon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The boundaries were fixed by agreements of 1898-1899, and the protectorate was formally established in 1901, with its capital later at <b>Tamale</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-territories-of-the-gold-coast">Northern Territories of the Gold Coast</a></li></ul></section>
